#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""PiNAPPLE HTTPS downgrade helper (a modern sslstrip for the Pi gateway).

PiNAPPLE already routes every AP client through the Pi, so the classic
sslstrip chain (arpspoof + iptables + sslstrip) reduces to one NAT REDIRECT:

    wlan0 client -> tcp/80 -> REDIRECT -> 10.0.0.1:10000 (this proxy)

The proxy keeps the client on plain HTTP while it talks TLS to the real
server, so the request line, headers and form bodies stay readable:

  * http://host/ answering 301/302/303/307/308 with an https:// Location is
    returned to the client as a plain-http redirect and the host is remembered
    as TLS-only. The browser's next request for the same URL is fetched
    upstream over TLS, so the address bar stays http:// and relative links and
    cookies keep working.
  * Location: https://...             -> http://...
  * Strict-Transport-Security         -> dropped (never pin HTTPS in a client)
  * Alt-Svc                            -> dropped (no HTTP/3 upgrade)
  * Set-Cookie: Secure                 -> un-Secured (cookie survives on http)
  * CSP upgrade-insecure-requests      -> dropped (subresources stay http)
  * https:// inside text bodies        -> http://
  * every request line, header and submitted form body is logged.

HTTP/3 (QUIC, udp/443) and TLS the client insists on by itself (HSTS preload,
HTTPS-First, a typed https:// URL) cannot be downgraded without a trusted
certificate, so udp/443 and tcp/443 are rejected by default. `--https allow`
leaves 443 alone: those flows stay encrypted and invisible, which is itself a
useful result to show.

Captive-network probes (captive.apple.com, connectivitycheck.gstatic.com, ...)
are answered locally so phones report "internet available" instead of opening
a captive sheet.

References: moxie0/sslstrip (Moxie Marlinspike, Black Hat DC 2009) for the
rewriting rules; bettercap's `https` module for the modern equivalent.
Python standard library only, like every other PiNAPPLE helper.

Usage:
  pinapple-strip on     [--https block|allow] [--port 10000] [--insecure]
  pinapple-strip off
  pinapple-strip status [--json]
  pinapple-strip log    [--json] [--lines 100]
  pinapple-strip clear
  pinapple-strip serve                       # internal / systemd ExecStart
"""

